    "answer": "Separation (delamination) in controlled-rolled steel is a phenomenon where brittle fracture occurs parallel to the plate surface, leading to a reduction in toughness. It is more pronounced in the through-thickness direction (Z direction) compared to the longitudinal direction (L direction), with a significant difference in transition temperature. This affects toughness by lowering the transition temperature and reducing the Charpy impact energy, making the material more brittle in the through-thickness direction. However, controlled rolling can also increase toughness in the longitudinal direction even in the ductile-fracture region, while reducing the occurrence of separation.",
